 "d": [
  {"i":3151,"keep":[],"n":"Police-raid sense is news vocabulary beyond N3; learners meet the care/maintenance sense."},
  {"i":3152,"keep":[3,4],"n":"ストレート勝ち (straight wins) is common in sports, and ストレートティー or whisky served straight is everyday drink vocabulary."},
  {"i":3183,"keep":[],"n":"Burglary sense (押し込み強盗) is rare at this level."},
  {"i":3187,"keep":[],"n":"Goal as an objective is a figurative extension; a teacher would not teach it in a thinking unit."},
  {"i":3195,"keep":[],"n":"'Field or sphere' is abstract and does not fit thinking; the direction/area sense is what learners need."},
  {"i":3197,"keep":[],"n":"On its own 状 rarely means letter; that sense lives in compounds like 年賀状 and 招待状, which are separate words."},
  {"i":3205,"keep":[],"n":"Clothespin is normally 洗濯ばさみ; ピンチ in that sense is rare for learners."},
  {"i":3208,"keep":[],"n":"意 meaning 'meaning' is a literary standalone use and not a language-unit word."},
  {"i":3213,"keep":[2],"n":"Tournament pairings (対戦の組み合わせ) are commonly met with sports."},
  {"i":3232,"keep":[],"n":"Origin sense (東京の出, 大学出) is not taught in a people unit."},
  {"i":3244,"keep":[2],"n":"Addressing a young woman as (お)ねえさん is common and fits people; senses 3 and 4 only duplicate it."},
  {"i":3247,"keep":[5],"n":"本体価格 appears on everyday price tags, so it is real shopping and money vocabulary."},
  {"i":3254,"keep":[],"n":"罪を着せる and 濡れ衣を着せる are figurative and beyond N3."},
  {"i":3255,"keep":[3],"n":"キャッチコピー (advertising slogan) is common media vocabulary; book-jacket blurb is rare."},
  {"i":3258,"keep":[5],"n":"一列, 二列 is a common counter use and belongs in measures."},
  {"i":3260,"keep":[3],"n":"はい、チーズ is the everyday photo phrase and fits the photography part of arts."},
  {"i":3263,"keep":[1,6],"n":"日が差す (sunlight) and 傘を差す (umbrella) are both standard weather-unit phrases."},
  {"i":3285,"keep":[1],"n":"The letter H belongs with the alphabet and writing systems."},
  {"i":3300,"keep":[],"n":"Trick or trap does not fit thinking; the device sense already covers the word."}
